# ripgrep configuration file.
#
# ripgrep does NOT look for a config file on its own. There is no
# "~/.ripgreprc is picked up automatically" behaviour. The only way ripgrep
# finds this file is the RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH environment variable, which
# laptonite exports from zsh/zshrc. If that export is missing, or if you are
# in a shell that never sourced ~/.zshrc, ripgrep silently ignores this file
# and falls back to its stock behaviour. That silence is the usual reason
# people think their ripgreprc "stopped working".
#
# FORMAT RULES (they are stricter than they look):
#   * One argument per line. `--smart-case` on its own line is fine.
#     `--smart-case --hidden` on one line is NOT -- ripgrep treats the whole
#     line as a single argument and errors out.
#   * For flags that take a value, put the value on the same line joined with
#     `=`, e.g. `--max-columns=150`. Do not use a space.
#   * Lines starting with `#` are comments. Blank lines are ignored.
#   * There is no shell quoting here. A line is passed through verbatim, so
#     quotes become part of the argument.
#
# Anything in this file behaves as if you typed it before your own arguments
# on the command line, so a flag you pass explicitly still wins.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# --smart-case: make `rg fkill` also match FKILL, FKill and fKiLl.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Out of the box ripgrep is case SENSITIVE, so `rg fkill` walks straight past
# a file containing FKILL. That is almost never what you want when you are
# searching from memory.
#
# --smart-case switches on the pattern you typed:
#
#   * Pattern is all lowercase  -> search is case INSENSITIVE.
#       rg fkill      matches  fkill, FKILL, FKill, fKiLl
#
#   * Pattern has any uppercase -> search is case SENSITIVE.
#       rg FKill      matches  FKill  only
#
# So you get the forgiving behaviour by default, and you opt back into an
# exact match simply by typing the capital letters you care about. There is
# no extra flag to remember in either direction.
#
# Escape hatches, when the automatic choice is wrong:
#   rg -s pattern    force case sensitive   (--case-sensitive)
#   rg -i pattern    force case insensitive (--ignore-case)
#
# Both override this file, because command line arguments are applied after
# the config file.
--smart-case
